<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nablus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Flavia Neapolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nablus is a city in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ebal">Mount Ebal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>, next to the site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>. It was founded as Flavia Neapolis by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 72 CE, and its name derives from Neapolis. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> it was a royal domain and lordship, and it later became a centre of soap-making and of the small Samaritan community.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nablus.jpg" alt="Nablus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: المُصوّر: مُعتز توفيق إغباريّة · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nablus_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
